Nope, putting a sack containing a Bag of Holding (or a BoT, or WoC) inside a 
Bag of holding cause it to blow up. But, ...

spoiler:


















Suppose you really, really, really want to make your bag of holding 
light.  The odds of a bag of holding being placed inside another 
bag of holding causing it to explode depend on the number of layers
which you place it in.  Seriously.

If you place a bag of holding inside a sack, inside a sack, inside a sack
inside a sack, inside a sack, inside a sack, inside a sack, inside another
bag of holding, there's a pretty good chance it won't explode.

Of course, if it does explode, you lose everything (both bags of holding,
all the sacks, all the contents, ...).  And everytime you want to take
anything out, you have to go through all the layers.  And everytime you
want to put it back in, you run the risk of it all exploding.


For those of you willing to run the risk, the odds (depending on
the number of recursions) are:

/* odds: 1/1, 2/2, 3/4, 4/8, 5/16, 6/32, 7/64, 8/128, 9/128, 10/128,... */

The optimal number seems to be using 7 sacks (gives 8/128 or 1/16).
Yes, I have tried this (in nh 3.1.0) and it does work.

What is the total weight reduction?  Ordinarily, the code says:

          *  The weight of bags of holding is calculated as the weight
          *  of the bag plus the weight of the bag's contents modified
          *  as follows:
          *
          *      Bag status      Weight of contents
          *      ----------      ------------------
          *      cursed                  2x
          *      blessed                 x/4 + 1
          *      otherwise               x/2 + 1

So a blessed bag of holding reduces the contained weight by about
a factor of 4.  If you manage to nest one inside another, the total
weight of the innermost stuff will be reduced by a factor of 16.

